    "created_at": "2015-12-07T10:54:57Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-12-07T10:54:57Z",
    "body": "I don't see legitimate use case for this. As a Web author, you should _always_ use UTF-8 and declare that you are using UTF-8. At that point, what's the use case for knowing what the default would have been?\n\nIf someone tries to use it for something, what non-harmful uses it could have? I can think of a harmful use case: trying to guess what encoding to use for downloadable text files. (Correct way: Use UTF-8 BOM for downloadable text/plain and use Python, etc. -specific encoding declarations for downloadable scripts with #!.)\n\nSo I see this as useless and harmful if used, so I think we shouldn't spec this.\n\nCan you tell if the Blink usage comes from \"let's check _everything_\" fingerprinting ad tech (not worth catering to)?\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/dom/issues/comments/162481999/reactions",
